@inject IDevToolService DevTools
@inject IServiceProvider Services
@implements IDisposable

@*
    The system tools pane: which Copilot plugins, repository tools, MCP servers
    and applications this machine is configured for, and what can be updated,
    enabled or disabled. It asks IDevToolService and renders the answer — which
    host is behind that port, and whether that host can do anything but read, is
    not something the pane knows.

    The Dashboard's shape (plan +system-tools-redesign, step 2): a header that names
    the tab and says in one sentence what is outstanding on it, the controls that
    scope the whole surface beside it, then one tab per kind of row. The kind used
    to be a heading in one long scroll; fifty-odd rows of four kinds read as a
    document nobody finishes, and "a plugin is behind" and "an application is
    missing" are two different afternoons. Applications split in two, because the
    rows confirmed by hand are a checklist somebody walks, not software anything
    can install. Overview (step 3) is the landing: the tab a machine with no
    remembered tab opens on, and the one place every kind is read at once.

    Every panel is Eager — rendered while another tab is shown — for the reason the
    Dashboard gives: switching back brings back what was there, and the strip's
    counts are the counts of rows that already exist rather than of rows a tab has
    not drawn yet. The kind toolbar is drawn in the active panel only, so its
    controls exist once.

    The host filter scopes every tab and every count: Claude means Claude Code or
    Claude Desktop, Copilot means Copilot, and the rows with no host at all —
    applications and the checklist — show only under All hosts. The tab and the
    filter are remembered per machine through IToolsViewPreferences, resolved
    optionally so a host that composes none still renders the pane.

    Groups with nothing outstanding fold themselves away: a correctly set-up machine
    should open a tab to cards and no rows.

    Three shapes of row rather than one. Most rows report a version and offer an
    Update or an Install. A checklist row has no version anywhere and reports
    whether it was detected. And a manual row reports nothing at all — nothing
    can check it, so its control is a box somebody ticks, and the box is the one
    control here that is not a verb.

    It owns its own loading for the same reason DevbookPane does: the list is
    the pane's subject, and a shell holding it would be holding state about a
    context it only makes room for. The shell decides *whether* there is a tools
    pane — the feature flag and the header toggle stay there — and this decides
    what is in one.

    Three states, not two. "There is no catalog file" and "the catalog is empty"
    used to render the same sentence and the same nothing-to-do, which made a
    fresh machine a dead end: the one act that would have helped — write the
    catalog — was not on the screen. CatalogExists is what tells them apart, and
    each gets the affordance that actually moves it on.

    Every editing affordance is behind CanEditCatalog. A host that answers this
    port read-only says so once, and the create, add, import and remove controls
    are simply not drawn rather than drawn and refused.
*@

<section @key=@("tools-pane") class="tools-panel" id="tools-pane" aria-labelledby="installed-tools-title" data-testid="tools-panel">
    @* The library's section header wearing this pane's class names, in the
       Dashboard's arrangement: eyebrow and headline on the left, the controls that
       scope the whole surface on the right, wrapping under the sentence when the
       pane is narrow. *@
    <SectionHeader BaseClass="tools-panel__header"
                   TextCssClass=""
                   EyebrowCssClass="tools-panel__eyebrow"
                   TitleCssClass="tools-panel__title"
                   DescriptionCssClass="tools-panel__subtitle"
                   DescriptionTestId="tools-checked"
                   ActionsCssClass="tools-panel__header-actions"
                   Eyebrow="@Eyebrow"
                   TitleId="installed-tools-title"
                   Title="@Headline"
                   Description="@CheckedLine"
                   TestId="tools-header">
        <Actions>
            @* A re-check over rows that are already on screen does not blank them,
               so the only place left to say that something is running is here. *@
            @if (_toolsLoading && _tools.Count > 0)
            {
                <Spinner Size="SpinnerSize.Small" Label="Checking tools" TestId="tools-refreshing" />
            }
            @* One choice of three, so a radio group: one stop in the tab order and
               the arrows move and select, as the Dashboard's window does. *@
            <ButtonGroup Radio="true" AriaLabel="Hosts to show" CssClass="tools-panel__hosts" TestId="tools-host-filter">
                @foreach (var host in HostFilters)
                {
                    <ToggleButton Pressed="@(_hostFilter == host.Id)"
                                  PressedChanged="_ => ChooseHostFilter(host.Id)"
                                  TestId="@($"tools-host-{host.Id}")">
                        @host.Label
                    </ToggleButton>
                }
            </ButtonGroup>
            @* Checking and updating both read the catalog, so neither is offered
               while there is no catalog to read. *@
            <AppButton Variant="ButtonVariant.Secondary" Disabled="@(!_catalogExists || _toolsLoading || ToolActionBusy)" OnClick="RefreshToolsAsync" TestId="tools-refresh">
                @(_toolsLoading ? "Checking..." : "Check for updates")
            </AppButton>
            <AppButton Variant="ButtonVariant.Primary" Disabled="@(!_catalogExists || _toolsLoading || ToolActionBusy || !CanUpdateAnyTool)" OnClick="UpdateAllToolsAsync" TestId="tools-update-all">
                @(_toolActionKey == UpdateAllKey ? "Updating..." : UpdateAllLabel)
            </AppButton>
            <IconButton AriaLabel="Close tools pane" CssClass="btn--ghost" OnClick="() => OnClose.InvokeAsync()">✕</IconButton>
        </Actions>
    </SectionHeader>

    @* The library's alert wearing this pane's message class. The fallback behind
       ToolMessage keeps it non-empty, so it never takes the alert's hidden shape. *@
    <Alert Message="@ToolMessage" BaseClass="tools-panel__message" Role="status" />

    <div class="tools-panel__content" id="installed-tools-content">
        @* Two columns rather than one stack, at the widths that have room for them:
           the inventory, and the transcript of the last run in a rail beside it.
           Below the width where both fit, the rail falls back under the list. *@
        <div class="tools-panel__main">
            @if (_toolsLoading && _tools.Count == 0)
            {
                @* The first read walks the machine — plugin manifests, marketplaces,
                   a version probe per application — and on a cold cache that is
                   several seconds of a pane with nothing in it. What stands in for
                   the inventory is the inventory's own shape: the same group card,
                   the same table, with bars where the rows will be. Hidden from
                   assistive technology in one piece: the status line above already
                   says the catalog is being checked, once. *@
                <div class="tools-panel__skeleton" data-testid="tools-loading" aria-hidden="true">
                    @foreach (var section in SkeletonSections)
                    {
                        <section class="tools-kind">
                            <Skeleton Shape="SkeletonShape.Heading" Width="8rem" />
                            <DataTable TItem="string"
                                       Columns="Columns"
                                       Items="section"
                                       RowNoun="tool"
                                       CssClass="tools-inventory">
                                <Row Context="name">
                                    <td class="tools-inventory__tool">
                                        <Skeleton Width="@name" />
                                        <Skeleton Width="55%" />
                                    </td>
                                    <td class="tools-inventory__version"><Skeleton Width="3rem" /></td>
                                    <td class="tools-inventory__version"><Skeleton Width="3rem" /></td>
                                    <td class="tools-inventory__status"><Skeleton Width="4rem" /></td>
                                    <td class="tools-inventory__actions-cell"><Skeleton Shape="SkeletonShape.Block" Width="5rem" /></td>
                                </Row>
                            </DataTable>
                        </section>
                    }
                </div>
            }
            else if (!_catalogExists)
            {
                <EmptyState Title="No tool catalog yet"
                            Description="@NoCatalogDescription"
                            Action="@NoCatalogActions"
                            TestId="tools-empty-no-catalog" />
            }
            else if (_tools.Count == 0)
            {
                <EmptyState Title="No tools in the catalog yet"
                            Description="@EmptyCatalogDescription"
                            Action="@EmptyCatalogActions"
                            TestId="tools-empty-no-entries" />
            }
            else
            {
                @* The library's tab strip: role="tablist", a roving tabindex, and the
                   arrow, Home and End keys. One row that scrolls sideways when the
                   pane is narrow, never a wrapped stack. *@
                <Tabs ActiveId="@_activeTab"
                      ActiveIdChanged="ChooseTab"
                      AriaLabel="Kinds of tool"
                      CssClass="tools-tabs"
                      StripCssClass="tools-tabs__strip"
                      PanelCssClass="tools-tabs__panels"
                      TestId="tools-tabs"
                      ListTestId="tools-tablist">
                    <ListTrailing>
                        @if (_commands.Count > 0)
                        {
                            @* A button rather than an anchor: it opens the transcript
                               fold in the rail rather than navigating anywhere. *@
                            <AppButton Variant="ButtonVariant.Ghost"
                                       CssClass="tools-tabs__transcript"
                                       OnClick="ShowTranscript"
                                       TestId="tools-transcript-link">
                                Last run transcript <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
                            </AppButton>
                        }
                    </ListTrailing>
                    <ChildContent>
                        <TabPanel Id="@OverviewTab" Title="Overview" Eager="true"
                                  BaseClass="tools-tab" TabTestId="tools-tab-overview" TestId="tools-overview">
                            @* The catalog's acts stay reachable from here: Overview is
                               the tab a machine opens on. *@
                            @if (_activeTab == OverviewTab)
                            {
                                @KindToolbar(OverviewTab)
                            }
                            @OverviewBody
                        </TabPanel>

                        @foreach (var tab in KindTabs)
                        {
                            var tabId = tab.Id;
                            <TabPanel @key="tabId" Id="@tabId" Title="@tab.Title" Eager="true"
                                      BaseClass="tools-tab" TabTestId="@($"tools-tab-{tab.Slug}")" TestId="@($"tools-{tab.Slug}")">
                                <TitleContent>
                                    @* Read from the pane's state when the strip draws it,
                                       not from the tab record it was built with, so the
                                       count is the current one even though the strip
                                       draws before this panel takes its parameters. *@
                                    @TabTitle(tabId)
                                    @if (TabAttention(tabId) is { Count: > 0 } attention)
                                    {
                                        <Badge Kind="tone"
                                               Slug="@attention.Tone"
                                               CssClass="tools-tabs__count"
                                               TestId="@($"tools-tab-{TabSlug(tabId)}-count")"
                                               aria-hidden="true">@attention.Count</Badge>
                                        <span class="sr-only">, @NeedActionSpoken(attention.Count)</span>
                                    }
                                </TitleContent>
                                <ChildContent>
                                    @if (_activeTab == tabId)
                                    {
                                        @KindToolbar(tabId)
                                    }
                                    @KindBody(tabId)
                                </ChildContent>
                            </TabPanel>
                        }
                    </ChildContent>
                </Tabs>
            }
        </div>

        @if (_commands.Count > 0)
        {
            @* Checking tools runs about a dozen processes, and this is the only
               trace of their output: a refused install or a search that could not
               reach nuget.org has nowhere else to be read. Folded, because it is
               diagnostics and not the pane's subject; the strip's "Last run
               transcript" opens it. A plain element and not a landmark: FoldControl
               already gives the transcript a labelled region. *@
            <div class="tools-panel__side" id="tools-transcript">
                <FoldControl CssClass="tools-panel__log"
                             Expanded="_commandsShown"
                             ExpandedChanged="shown => _commandsShown = shown"
                             Label="@($"Command output ({_commands.Count})")"
                             TestId="tools-command-log">
                    <CodeView Source="@CommandLog" Language="console" TestId="tools-command-log-output" />
                </FoldControl>
            </div>
        }
    </div>
</section>

<Modal Open="@_addOpen"
       OpenChanged="OnAddOpenChanged"
       Title="Add a tool"
       CssClass="tools-dialog"
       TestId="tools-add-dialog">
    <ChildContent>
        <div class="tools-form">
            @* SelectField rather than EnumSelect: the enum member is McpServer and
               the humanised form of that reads "Mcp Server", which is not what the
               product calls it anywhere else. *@
            @* Form, like every field below it: the two selects at the top of this
               dialog are the ones that decide which of those fields exist, so of
               everything here they are the last things that should read as
               captions rather than as controls. *@
            <SelectField Label="Kind"
                         Form="true"
                         CurrentValue="@_draftKind.ToString()"
                         Options="@KindOptions"
                         HelpText="What the tool is. Changing it changes what the form asks for below."
                         TestId="tools-add-kind"
                         OnChanged="OnDraftKindChanged" />

            @if (_draftKind is DevToolKind.Plugin or DevToolKind.McpServer)
            {
                @* Named kinds rather than "not a marketplace", because the list
                   grew a third kind that has no hosts either. A marketplace is a
                   Claude mechanism and nothing else; an application is installed
                   into the machine rather than into an AI host. Offering either of
                   them the choice would be offering an answer that cannot be
                   given — and the condition this replaced would have offered it to
                   applications silently. *@
                <SelectField Label="Hosts"
                             Form="true"
                             CurrentValue="@_draftHosts.ToString()"
                             Options="@HostOptions"
                             HelpText="Which host CLIs it is installed for. Including Claude adds the fields it needs."
                             TestId="tools-add-hosts"
                             OnChanged="OnDraftHostsChanged" />
            }

            @if (_draftKind is DevToolKind.Marketplace)
            {
                <TextField Label="Name"
                           Value="@_draftMarketplaceName"
                           ValueChanged="value => _draftMarketplaceName = value"
                           HelpText="What Claude will call it — the marketplace half of a plugin id."
                           ErrorMessage="@_draftIdError"
                           TestId="tools-add-name" />
                <TextField Label="Source"
                           Value="@_draftSource"
                           ValueChanged="value => _draftSource = value"
                           HelpText="The repository it is added from: an owner/repo, or a URL."
                           ErrorMessage="@_draftSourceError"
                           TestId="tools-add-source" />
            }
            else if (_draftKind is DevToolKind.Plugin)
            {
                <TextField Label="Name"
                           Value="@_draftPluginName"
                           ValueChanged="value => _draftPluginName = value"
                           HelpText="The plugin's name, exactly as its host CLI lists it. Claude's own name for it, when it differs, goes below."
                           ErrorMessage="@_draftIdError"
                           TestId="tools-add-name" />
                <TextField Label="Source"
                           Value="@_draftSource"
                           ValueChanged="value => _draftSource = value"
                           HelpText="Where it is installed from: an owner/repo:path, or a URL."
                           ErrorMessage="@_draftSourceError"
                           TestId="tools-add-source" />
                <SelectField Label="Kind of plugin"
                             Form="true"
                             CurrentValue="@_draftPluginKind"
                             Options="@PluginKindOptions"
                             IncludeEmptyOption="true"
                             EmptyLabel="Plugin (default)"
                             HelpText="Optional. Only the kinds the desktop host branches on; a plain plugin needs none."
                             TestId="tools-add-plugin-kind"
                             OnChanged="value => _draftPluginKind = value ?? string.Empty" />

                @if (_draftHosts.HasFlag(DevToolHosts.Claude))
                {
                    @* Both blank in the ordinary case: a plugin's Claude id falls
                       back to its own name and to the first marketplace in the
                       catalog, and writing either out when it only restates the
                       fallback makes the catalog harder to read. *@
                    <TextField Label="Claude name"
                               Value="@_draftClaudeName"
                               ValueChanged="value => _draftClaudeName = value"
                               HelpText="Optional. Only when the marketplace calls it something else."
                               TestId="tools-add-claude-name" />
                    <TextField Label="Claude marketplace"
                               Value="@_draftClaudeMarketplace"
                               ValueChanged="value => _draftClaudeMarketplace = value"
                               HelpText="Optional. Defaults to the first marketplace in the catalog."
                               TestId="tools-add-claude-marketplace" />
                }
            }
            else if (_draftKind is DevToolKind.Application)
            {
                @* An explicit branch, and not because it needs different fields —
                   because the chain it joins is binary-exhaustive and falls
                   through to the MCP server. Without this, choosing Application
                   would have drawn a package id and a Claude registration, and
                   submitted whatever was typed into them as an application. *@
                @* Form, like the two selects above it. This is the third of the
                   dialog's mode switches — it decides whether the command fields
                   below exist — and a switch that reads as a caption is the thing
                   the form shape was added for. *@
                <SelectField Label="Installed by"
                             Form="true"
                             CurrentValue="@_draftProvider.ToString()"
                             Options="@ProviderOptions"
                             HelpText="How this machine gets it. Choosing a command asks for the probe to run."
                             TestId="tools-add-provider"
                             OnChanged="OnDraftProviderChanged" />
                <TextField Label="ID"
                           Value="@_draftApplicationId"
                           ValueChanged="value => _draftApplicationId = value"
                           HelpText="@ApplicationIdHelp"
                           ErrorMessage="@_draftIdError"
                           TestId="tools-add-app-id" />
                <TextField Label="Name"
                           Value="@_draftDisplayName"
                           ValueChanged="value => _draftDisplayName = value"
                           HelpText="Optional. What the row reads as; defaults to the id."
                           TestId="tools-add-name" />

                @if (_draftProvider is DevToolProvider.Command)
                {
                    @* The other three mechanisms know how to find their own
                       package. A command entry is the one that has to be told, and
                       one that was not can never answer whether the machine has
                       it — so the detect command is required here rather than
                       written and drawn forever as a row of unknowns. *@
                    <TextField Label="Detect command"
                               Value="@_draftDetectCommand"
                               ValueChanged="value => _draftDetectCommand = value"
                               HelpText="What answers whether this machine already has it."
                               ErrorMessage="@_draftDetectError"
                               TestId="tools-add-detect-command" />
                    <TextField Label="Detect arguments"
                               Value="@_draftDetectArgs"
                               ValueChanged="value => _draftDetectArgs = value"
                               HelpText="Optional. Separated by spaces, in the order they are passed."
                               TestId="tools-add-detect-args" />
                    <TextField Label="Expect"
                               Value="@_draftDetectExpect"
                               ValueChanged="value => _draftDetectExpect = value"
                               HelpText="Optional. The substring that means yes, when the command answers in prose rather than with a version."
                               TestId="tools-add-detect-expect" />
                    <TextField Label="Install command"
                               Value="@_draftInstallCommand"
                               ValueChanged="value => _draftInstallCommand = value"
                               HelpText="Leave blank for a checklist item: something worth looking for that is not ours to press a button about."
                               TestId="tools-add-install-command" />
                    <TextField Label="Install arguments"
                               Value="@_draftInstallArgs"
                               ValueChanged="value => _draftInstallArgs = value"
                               HelpText="Optional. Separated by spaces, in the order they are passed."
                               TestId="tools-add-install-args" />
                }
            }
            else
            {
                @* The dialog's fourth mode switch, and the reason this branch grew
                   one: the mcpServers array holds two kinds of server, and which of
                   them is being added decides what the id below even is. A .NET
                   tool is identified by its package id and a command-registered
                   server by its name, so a form that only ever asked for a package
                   id could not write the second kind at all — and the entry it did
                   write would be keyed on a property nothing reads. *@
                <SelectField Label="Registered by"
                             Form="true"
                             CurrentValue="@_draftMcpMechanism.ToString()"
                             Options="@McpMechanismOptions"
                             HelpText="How this machine reaches the server. A .NET tool is installed; a command is already there and is started; a URL is reached over HTTP."
                             TestId="tools-add-mcp-mechanism"
                             OnChanged="OnDraftMcpMechanismChanged" />
            }

            @if (_draftKind is DevToolKind.McpServer && _draftMcpMechanism is DevToolMcpMechanism.Http)
            {
                @* The third shape, and the only one whose name is not decoration:
                   it is the first positional argument of
                   `claude mcp add --transport http`, so there is no registering
                   this entry without one. No Claude section either, for the same
                   reason the command shape has none — the URL is what every host
                   this entry targets is registered with. *@
                <TextField Label="Name"
                           Value="@_draftServerName"
                           ValueChanged="value => _draftServerName = value"
                           HelpText="What the server is registered as. It is what the row and any per-PC override are addressed by."
                           ErrorMessage="@_draftIdError"
                           TestId="tools-add-server-name" />
                <TextField Label="URL"
                           Value="@_draftServerUrl"
                           ValueChanged="value => _draftServerUrl = value"
                           HelpText="Where the server answers. ${BACKLOG_MCP_PORT} and ${BACKLOG_MCP_TOKEN} are filled in per machine; nothing else is, and never an environment variable."
                           ErrorMessage="@_draftCommandError"
                           TestId="tools-add-server-url" />
                <TextArea Label="Headers"
                          Value="@_draftServerHeaders"
                          ValueChanged="value => _draftServerHeaders = value"
                          HelpText="Optional. One per line, as Name: value — in the order they are sent."
                          TestId="tools-add-server-headers" />
            }
            else if (_draftKind is DevToolKind.McpServer && _draftMcpMechanism is DevToolMcpMechanism.Command)
            {
                @* No package id, and no Claude section either: the command is what
                   every host this entry targets is registered with, and a section
                   that only restated it would be a second place for it to drift. *@
                <TextField Label="Name"
                           Value="@_draftServerName"
                           ValueChanged="value => _draftServerName = value"
                           HelpText="What the server is called. It is what the row and any per-PC override are addressed by."
                           ErrorMessage="@_draftIdError"
                           TestId="tools-add-server-name" />
                <TextField Label="Command"
                           Value="@_draftServerCommand"
                           ValueChanged="value => _draftServerCommand = value"
                           HelpText="The executable that starts the server."
                           ErrorMessage="@_draftCommandError"
                           TestId="tools-add-server-command" />
                <TextField Label="Command arguments"
                           Value="@_draftServerArgs"
                           ValueChanged="value => _draftServerArgs = value"
                           HelpText="Optional. Separated by spaces, in the order they are passed."
                           TestId="tools-add-server-args" />
            }
            else if (_draftKind is DevToolKind.McpServer)
            {
                <TextField Label="Package ID"
                           Value="@_draftPackageId"
                           ValueChanged="value => _draftPackageId = value"
                           HelpText="The .NET tool package the server ships as."
                           ErrorMessage="@_draftIdError"
                           TestId="tools-add-package-id" />
                <TextField Label="Name"
                           Value="@_draftDisplayName"
                           ValueChanged="value => _draftDisplayName = value"
                           HelpText="Optional. What the row reads as beside the package id."
                           TestId="tools-add-name" />

                @if (_draftHosts.HasFlag(DevToolHosts.Claude))
                {
                    @* The .NET tool above is shared by both hosts. These three are
                       the extra step Claude needs on top of it: a user-scope
                       registration pointing at the executable the tool installs. *@
                    <TextField Label="Claude server name"
                               Value="@_draftClaudeServerName"
                               ValueChanged="value => _draftClaudeServerName = value"
                               HelpText="Optional. What 'claude mcp' registers it as; defaults to the name above."
                               TestId="tools-add-claude-server-name" />
                    <TextField Label="Claude command"
                               Value="@_draftClaudeCommand"
                               ValueChanged="value => _draftClaudeCommand = value"
                               HelpText="The executable the tool installs. Leave blank to install the tool without registering it."
                               TestId="tools-add-claude-command" />
                    <TextField Label="Claude arguments"
                               Value="@_draftClaudeArgs"
                               ValueChanged="value => _draftClaudeArgs = value"
                               HelpText="Optional. Separated by spaces, in the order they are passed."
                               TestId="tools-add-claude-args" />
                }
            }
        </div>
    </ChildContent>
    <Footer>
        <AppButton Variant="ButtonVariant.Ghost" OnClick="CloseAddDialog" TestId="tools-add-cancel">Cancel</AppButton>
        <AppButton Variant="ButtonVariant.Primary" Disabled="@ToolActionBusy" OnClick="SubmitAddAsync" TestId="tools-add-submit">
            @(_toolActionKey == AddKey ? "Adding..." : "Add tool")
        </AppButton>
    </Footer>
</Modal>

<Modal Open="@_importOpen"
       OpenChanged="OnImportOpenChanged"
       Title="Import a tool catalog"
       CssClass="tools-dialog"
       TestId="tools-import-dialog">
    <ChildContent>
        <div class="tools-form">
            <p class="tools-form__note">
                The catalog is replaced, not merged. The one that is there now is kept beside it as a .bak file.
            </p>
            <FileField Label="Catalog file"
                       Accept=".json"
                       HelpText="An ai-tools.json with a plugins, mcpServers, claude.marketplaces or applications array."
                       ErrorMessage="@_importError"
                       OnTextRead="OnImportFileRead"
                       OnRejected="OnImportFileRejected"
                       TestId="tools-import-file" />
            @* The paste box is not a fallback for a broken picker: a catalog
               arrives as often in a message or a review as it does as a file on
               this machine, and both end at the same ImportAsync(string). *@
            <TextArea Label="Or paste the JSON"
                      Value="@_importJson"
                      ValueChanged="value => _importJson = value"
                      AutoGrow="false"
                      Rows="8"
                      Placeholder="@ImportPlaceholder"
                      TestId="tools-import-json" />
        </div>
    </ChildContent>
    <Footer>
        <AppButton Variant="ButtonVariant.Ghost" OnClick="CloseImportDialog">Cancel</AppButton>
        <AppButton Variant="ButtonVariant.Primary" Disabled="@ToolActionBusy" OnClick="SubmitImportAsync" TestId="tools-import-submit">
            @(_toolActionKey == ImportKey ? "Importing..." : "Replace catalog")
        </AppButton>
    </Footer>
</Modal>

@* No OpenChanged binding, deliberately. ConfirmDialog raises it before OnConfirm,
   so a handler that cleared the subject there would clear it a moment before the
   confirm needed it. Cancel, Escape and the backdrop all arrive on OnCancel, which
   is the one path that has to forget what was being removed. *@
<ConfirmDialog Open="@(_removing is not null)"
               Title="Remove this tool?"
               Message="@RemoveMessage"
               ConfirmText="Remove"
               Destructive="true"
               TestId="tools-remove-dialog"
               ConfirmTestId="tools-remove-confirm"
               CancelTestId="tools-remove-cancel"
               OnConfirm="ConfirmRemoveAsync"
               OnCancel="CancelRemove" />

@* The two cache deletes ask first, as the catalog Remove above does. They are
   the one thing on this pane that deletes folders under the person's profile,
   recursively and with no way back, and a first-click delete is one misread
   row from taking a version an install still loads. *@
<ConfirmDialog Open="@(_cacheRemoval is not null)"
               Title="@CacheRemovalTitle"
               Message="@CacheRemovalMessage"
               ConfirmText="Remove"
               Destructive="true"
               TestId="tools-cache-remove-dialog"
               ConfirmTestId="tools-cache-remove-confirm"
               CancelTestId="tools-cache-remove-cancel"
               OnConfirm="ConfirmCacheRemovalAsync"
               OnCancel="CancelCacheRemoval" />

@* The last run's whole transcript, which the Overview's Last run card shows the
   tail of. A dialog rather than the rail's fold: the fold is the transcript of
   whatever this pane last ran or checked, and after a restart that is a check,
   not the run the card is about. *@
<Modal Open="@_lastRunTranscriptOpen"
       OpenChanged="open => _lastRunTranscriptOpen = open"
       Title="Last run transcript"
       CssClass="tools-dialog"
       TestId="tools-last-run-transcript-dialog">
    <ChildContent>
        <CodeView Source="@(_lastRun?.Transcript ?? string.Empty)" Language="console" TestId="tools-last-run-transcript" />
    </ChildContent>
    <Footer>
        <AppButton Variant="ButtonVariant.Secondary" OnClick="() => _lastRunTranscriptOpen = false">Close</AppButton>
    </Footer>
</Modal>

@code {
    // ------------------------------------------------------------ tabs and filters

    /// <summary>The Overview tab's panel id.</summary>
    private const string OverviewTab = "tools-overview";

    /// <summary>The tab a machine opens on before it has been on any: the
    /// Overview, which reads every kind at once.</summary>
    private const string DefaultTab = OverviewTab;

    private const string AllHosts = "all";
    private const string ClaudeHosts = "claude";
    private const string CopilotHosts = "copilot";

    /// <summary>The panel on show, by its id.</summary>
    private string _activeTab = DefaultTab;

    /// <summary>The host filter, by its id.</summary>
    private string _hostFilter = AllHosts;

    /// <summary>When the last listing came back, for the header's muted line. Null
    /// until one has.</summary>
    private DateTimeOffset? _checkedAt;

    /// <summary>Per tab, whether its toolbar narrows it to the rows that need
    /// action. All by default; never remembered — a filter on what is outstanding
    /// today is not a preference.</summary>
    private readonly Dictionary<string, bool> _needsActionOnly = new(StringComparer.Ordinal);

    /// <summary>Per tab, the text its name filter holds.</summary>
    private readonly Dictionary<string, string> _nameFilters = new(StringComparer.Ordinal);

    /// <summary>Where the tab and the host filter are remembered, when the host
    /// composed anywhere. Resolved optionally, the way the Dashboard resolves its
    /// clock: a host that registers none still renders the pane, and the choices
    /// then last as long as it.</summary>
    private IToolsViewPreferences? Preferences => Services.GetService(typeof(IToolsViewPreferences)) as IToolsViewPreferences;

    private TimeProvider Time => Services.GetService(typeof(TimeProvider)) as TimeProvider ?? TimeProvider.System;

    /// <summary>One kind tab: its panel id, the slug its test ids and the
    /// remembered value use, its title, the kind attribute its section carries, and
    /// which rows it holds.</summary>
    private sealed record KindTab(string Id, string Slug, string Title, string KindKey, string Noun, string NounOne, Func<DevToolInfo, bool> Holds);

    /// <summary>The kind tabs in the order the strip draws them. Applications are
    /// two tabs: the rows confirmed by hand are a checklist somebody walks, and
    /// filed with the software they read as rows that failed to install.</summary>
    private static readonly KindTab[] KindTabs =
    [
        new("tools-plugins", "plugins", "Plugins", nameof(DevToolKind.Plugin), "plugins", "plugin",
            tool => tool.Kind is DevToolKind.Plugin),
        new("tools-marketplaces", "marketplaces", "Marketplaces", nameof(DevToolKind.Marketplace), "marketplaces", "marketplace",
            tool => tool.Kind is DevToolKind.Marketplace),
        new("tools-mcp-servers", "mcp-servers", "MCP servers", nameof(DevToolKind.McpServer), "MCP servers", "MCP server",
            tool => tool.Kind is DevToolKind.McpServer),
        new("tools-applications", "applications", "Applications", nameof(DevToolKind.Application), "applications", "application",
            tool => tool.Kind is DevToolKind.Application && !tool.ConfirmedByHand),
        new("tools-checklist", "checklist", "Checklist", "Checklist", "checklist items", "checklist item",
            tool => tool.Kind is DevToolKind.Application && tool.ConfirmedByHand)
    ];

    private static readonly (string Id, string Label)[] HostFilters =
    [
        (AllHosts, "All hosts"),
        (ClaudeHosts, "Claude"),
        (CopilotHosts, "Copilot")
    ];

    private static KindTab? KindTabOf(string tabId) => Array.Find(KindTabs, tab => tab.Id == tabId);

    private static string TabSlug(string tabId) => KindTabOf(tabId)?.Slug ?? "overview";

    private static string TabTitle(string tabId) => KindTabOf(tabId)?.Title ?? "Overview";

    private static bool IsKnownTab(string tabId) => tabId == OverviewTab || KindTabOf(tabId) is not null;

    /// <summary>Reads back the tab and the filter this machine last had. A value
    /// this build does not know — a tab a later build added, a hand edit — falls
    /// back to the default rather than to a tab with nothing behind it.</summary>
    private void RestoreView()
    {
        if (Preferences is not { } preferences) return;

        if (preferences.Tab is { Length: > 0 } tab && IsKnownTab("tools-" + tab))
        {
            _activeTab = "tools-" + tab;
        }

        if (preferences.HostFilter is AllHosts or ClaudeHosts or CopilotHosts)
        {
            _hostFilter = preferences.HostFilter;
        }
    }

    private void ChooseTab(string tabId)
    {
        _activeTab = tabId;
        Preferences?.SetTab(TabSlug(tabId));
    }

    private void ChooseHostFilter(string hostFilter)
    {
        _hostFilter = hostFilter;
        Preferences?.SetHostFilter(hostFilter);
    }

    /// <summary>Opens the transcript the strip's link names.</summary>
    private void ShowTranscript() => _commandsShown = true;

    /// <summary>Whether a row is for the hosts the filter shows. Claude is Claude
    /// Code or Claude Desktop; a row with no host — an application, a checklist
    /// item — is machine software rather than a registration with either, so it
    /// shows only under All hosts.</summary>
    private bool MatchesHost(DevToolInfo tool) => _hostFilter switch
    {
        ClaudeHosts => (tool.Hosts & (DevToolHosts.Claude | DevToolHosts.ClaudeDesktop)) != DevToolHosts.None,
        CopilotHosts => (tool.Hosts & DevToolHosts.Copilot) != DevToolHosts.None,
        _ => true
    };

    /// <summary>Every row the host filter lets through, in catalog order.</summary>
    private IEnumerable<DevToolInfo> HostRows => _tools.Where(MatchesHost);

    /// <summary>The rows a tab holds under the host filter, before its own
    /// toolbar narrows them. What its counts are counted over.</summary>
    private IReadOnlyList<DevToolInfo> RowsOn(string tabId) => KindTabOf(tabId) is { } tab
        ? HostRows.Where(tab.Holds).ToArray()
        : HostRows.ToArray();

    private bool NeedsActionOnly(string tabId) => _needsActionOnly.TryGetValue(tabId, out var only) && only;

    private void SetNeedsActionOnly(string tabId, bool only) => _needsActionOnly[tabId] = only;

    private string NameFilter(string tabId) => _nameFilters.TryGetValue(tabId, out var text) ? text : string.Empty;

    private void SetNameFilter(string tabId, string? text) => _nameFilters[tabId] = text ?? string.Empty;

    /// <summary>The rows a tab draws: its rows, narrowed by its toolbar.</summary>
    private IEnumerable<DevToolInfo> VisibleRowsOn(string tabId)
    {
        var rows = RowsOn(tabId).AsEnumerable();

        if (NeedsActionOnly(tabId))
        {
            rows = rows.Where(NeedsAttention);
        }

        var text = NameFilter(tabId).Trim();
        if (text.Length > 0)
        {
            rows = rows.Where(tool => tool.Name.Contains(text, StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase));
        }

        return rows;
    }

    /// <summary>How many rows on a tab need action.</summary>
    private int AttentionCount(string tabId) => RowsOn(tabId).Count(NeedsAttention);

    /// <summary>What a tab's badge says and in which tone: the count of rows that
    /// need action, toned by the worst of them — error for a missing row, warning
    /// for one that is behind, neutral for one nobody has confirmed. Null while
    /// nothing on the tab needs action, which draws no badge rather than a zero.</summary>
    private (int Count, string Tone)? TabAttention(string tabId)
    {
        var states = RowsOn(tabId).Select(StateOf).Where(IsOutstanding).ToArray();

        if (states.Length == 0) return null;

        var tone = states.Contains(ToolState.Missing) ? "fault"
            : states.Contains(ToolState.Update) ? "alert"
            : "quiet";

        return (states.Length, tone);
    }

    private static string NeedActionSpoken(int count) =>
        count == 1 ? "1 needs action" : $"{count} need action";

    /// <summary>The cards a tab draws: one per catalog group, in the order the
    /// groups first appear, over the rows its toolbar leaves.</summary>
    private IReadOnlyList<ToolGroup> GroupsOn(string tabId)
    {
        var tab = KindTabOf(tabId);
        if (tab is null) return [];

        var rows = VisibleRowsOn(tabId).ToArray();
        var named = rows.Any(tool => !string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(tool.Group));

        return rows
            .GroupBy(tool => string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(tool.Group) ? null : tool.Group.Trim())
            .Select(group => new ToolGroup(
                // The ungrouped bucket's key ends at the colon: a catalog group is
                // never blank, so no group name can produce it.
                $"{tab.KindKey}:{group.Key}",
                group.Key ?? (named ? "Other" : tab.Title),
                group.ToArray()))
            .ToArray();
    }

    private string EmptyTabTitle(string tabId) => RowsOn(tabId).Count == 0
        ? $"No {KindTabOf(tabId)?.Noun ?? "rows"} here"
        : "Nothing matches";

    private string EmptyTabDescription(string tabId) => RowsOn(tabId).Count == 0
        ? _hostFilter == AllHosts
            ? "The catalog has none of these for this machine."
            : "None of these are for the hosts the filter shows."
        : "No row on this tab matches the toolbar's filters.";

    // ------------------------------------------------------------ the header

    private string Eyebrow => "System tools · " + TabTitle(_activeTab);

    /// <summary>
    /// The header's sentence: what is outstanding on the tab on show, filled from a
    /// fixed template — "Two of eighteen plugins are behind", or "Everything matches
    /// the catalog" when nothing is. A header is never a loading state, so until
    /// the first listing lands it names the pane's state instead.
    /// </summary>
    private string Headline
    {
        get
        {
            if (_toolsLoading && _tools.Count == 0) return "Checking this machine";
            if (!_catalogExists) return "No tool catalog yet";
            if (_tools.Count == 0) return "The catalog is empty";

            var rows = RowsOn(_activeTab);
            var tab = KindTabOf(_activeTab);
            var states = rows.Select(StateOf).Where(IsOutstanding).ToArray();

            // A tab the filter has emptied says so, rather than claiming that rows
            // it hides match the catalog.
            if (rows.Count == 0)
            {
                var none = tab?.Noun ?? "entries";
                return _hostFilter == AllHosts ? $"No {none} in the catalog" : $"No {none} for these hosts";
            }

            if (states.Length == 0) return "Everything matches the catalog";

            var noun = tab is null ? (rows.Count == 1 ? "entry" : "entries") : rows.Count == 1 ? tab.NounOne : tab.Noun;
            var one = states.Length == 1;
            var finding = states.Distinct().ToArray() switch
            {
                [ToolState.Update] => one ? "is behind" : "are behind",
                [ToolState.Missing] => one ? "is missing" : "are missing",
                [ToolState.NotConfirmed] => one ? "is not confirmed" : "are not confirmed",
                _ => one ? "needs action" : "need action"
            };

            // Both numbers in words or both in digits: "Fourteen of 22" reads as two
            // registers in one sentence.
            var words = rows.Count < SmallNumbers.Length;
            return Capitalised($"{NumberWord(states.Length, words)} of {NumberWord(rows.Count, words)} {noun} {finding}");
        }
    }

    /// <summary>The muted line under the sentence: when the catalog was last
    /// checked, and how many entries the tab on show holds.</summary>
    private string CheckedLine
    {
        get
        {
            if (_toolsLoading && _tools.Count == 0) return "Checking the catalog";
            if (!_catalogExists) return "No catalog on this machine";

            var count = RowsOn(_activeTab).Count;
            var entries = count == 1 ? "1 entry" : $"{count} entries";

            return _checkedAt is { } at
                ? $"Checked {TimeZoneInfo.ConvertTime(at, Time.LocalTimeZone):HH:mm} · {entries}"
                : entries;
        }
    }

    private static readonly string[] SmallNumbers =
    [
        "zero", "one", "two", "three", "four", "five", "six", "seven", "eight", "nine", "ten",
        "eleven", "twelve", "thirteen", "fourteen", "fifteen", "sixteen", "seventeen", "eighteen", "nineteen", "twenty"
    ];

    /// <summary>A count as the headline says it: in words up to twenty, the way
    /// a sentence would, and in digits past it.</summary>
    private static string NumberWord(int count, bool words) =>
        words && count >= 0 && count < SmallNumbers.Length ? SmallNumbers[count] : count.ToString(System.Globalization.CultureInfo.InvariantCulture);

    private static string Capitalised(string text) => text.Length == 0 ? text : char.ToUpperInvariant(text[0]) + text[1..];

    /// <summary>How many rows Update all would act on. Not narrowed by the host
    /// filter: the port's Update all updates every row, and a count that left
    /// some of them out would promise less than the button does.</summary>
    private int UpdatableCount =>
        _tools.Count(tool => tool.CanUpdate || (tool.Kind is DevToolKind.Marketplace && !tool.Installed));

    private string UpdateAllLabel => UpdatableCount > 0 ? $"Update all ({UpdatableCount})" : "Update all";

    // ------------------------------------------------------------ row state

    /// <summary>What a row's status chip says: one word for where the row stands,
    /// which is also what decides whether it needs action.</summary>
    private enum ToolState
    {
        Current,
        Update,
        Missing,
        Off,
        Confirmed,
        NotConfirmed,
        Unknown
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Where a row stands.
    ///
    /// <para>Switched off comes first: a row this machine has said it does not
    /// want wants nothing, whatever it reports. Then the two shapes with no version
    /// to compare — a row confirmed by hand, a marketplace — and then the version
    /// rows, where a drifted registration is an update like a newer version is:
    /// both are a press of the row's one verb away from matching the catalog.</para>
    ///
    /// <para>Wider than the rule before the tabs, which counted only a row with an
    /// Update or an Install to press. A row that is missing is missing whether or not
    /// anything here can install it — an install by hand, a server registered by a
    /// command, a checklist probe that found nothing — and a Needs action that hid it
    /// would hide the one row somebody has to go and do.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private static ToolState StateOf(DevToolInfo tool) => tool switch
    {
        { ConfiguredEnabled: false } => ToolState.Off,
        { ConfirmedByHand: true } => tool.Acknowledged ? ToolState.Confirmed : ToolState.NotConfirmed,
        { Kind: DevToolKind.Marketplace } => tool.Installed ? ToolState.Current : ToolState.Missing,
        _ when tool.CanUpdate || tool.CanReRegister => ToolState.Update,
        _ when tool.CanInstall => ToolState.Missing,
        _ when ReportsDetection(tool) => tool.Installed ? ToolState.Current : ToolState.Missing,
        { Installed: false } => ToolState.Missing,
        { AvailableVersionKnown: false } => ToolState.Unknown,
        _ => ToolState.Current
    };

    private static bool IsOutstanding(ToolState state) =>
        state is ToolState.Update or ToolState.Missing or ToolState.NotConfirmed;

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether a row still wants something done about it — what the tab badges
    /// count, what Needs action narrows to, and what decides which groups open by
    /// themselves. A machine that is set up correctly should open a tab to folded
    /// cards and no rows.
    /// </summary>
    private static bool NeedsAttention(DevToolInfo tool) => IsOutstanding(StateOf(tool));

    private static string StateLabel(ToolState state) => state switch
    {
        ToolState.Current => "Up to date",
        ToolState.Update => "Update",
        ToolState.Missing => "Missing",
        ToolState.Off => "Off here",
        ToolState.Confirmed => "Confirmed by hand",
        ToolState.NotConfirmed => "Not confirmed",
        ToolState.Unknown => "Unknown"
    };

    /// <summary>The chip's tone, from the library's tone family
    /// (color-scheme.md#badge-and-chip-tones).</summary>
    private static string StateTone(ToolState state) => state switch
    {
        ToolState.Current or ToolState.Confirmed => "settled",
        ToolState.Update => "alert",
        ToolState.Missing => "fault",
        ToolState.Off => "archived",
        ToolState.NotConfirmed or ToolState.Unknown => "quiet"
    };

    /// <summary>The hosts a row names, one chip each, in a fixed order.</summary>
    private static IEnumerable<DevToolHosts> HostsOf(DevToolHosts hosts) =>
        new[] { DevToolHosts.Copilot, DevToolHosts.Claude, DevToolHosts.ClaudeDesktop }.Where(host => hosts.HasFlag(host));

    // ------------------------------------------------------------ the kind tab body

    /// <summary>The toolbar over a tab: the Needs action toggle and the name
    /// filter on a kind tab, and the acts that edit the catalog on every tab. Drawn
    /// in the active panel only, so each control exists once.</summary>
    private RenderFragment KindToolbar(string tabId) =>
        @<div class="tools-panel__toolbar" data-testid="tools-kind-toolbar">
            @if (KindTabOf(tabId) is not null)
            {
                <ButtonGroup Radio="true" AriaLabel="Rows to show" CssClass="tools-panel__scope" TestId="tools-needs-filter">
                    <ToggleButton Pressed="@NeedsActionOnly(tabId)"
                                  PressedChanged="_ => SetNeedsActionOnly(tabId, true)"
                                  TestId="tools-needs-action">@($"Needs action ({AttentionCount(tabId)})")</ToggleButton>
                    <ToggleButton Pressed="@(!NeedsActionOnly(tabId))"
                                  PressedChanged="_ => SetNeedsActionOnly(tabId, false)"
                                  TestId="tools-needs-all">@($"All ({RowsOn(tabId).Count})")</ToggleButton>
                </ButtonGroup>
                <TextField AriaLabel="Filter by name"
                           Compact="true"
                           Placeholder="Filter by name"
                           Value="@NameFilter(tabId)"
                           ValueChanged="value => SetNameFilter(tabId, value)"
                           CssClass="tools-panel__filter"
                           TestId="tools-name-filter" />
            }
            <div class="tools-panel__toolbar-actions">
                @* Not on the Overview, where the stale cache is a Needs you card
                   with a Clear of its own. *@
                @if (StaleCacheCount > 0 && tabId != OverviewTab)
                {
                    @* Only while some row reports a folder nothing loads: a button
                       that appears is the pane saying there is something to clear. *@
                    <AppButton Variant="ButtonVariant.Secondary"
                               Disabled="@(_toolsLoading || ToolActionBusy)"
                               OnClick="AskToClearStaleCache"
                               TestId="tools-cache-clear">
                        @(_toolActionKey == ClearCacheKey ? "Clearing..." : $"Clear stale cache ({StaleCacheCount})")
                    </AppButton>
                }
                @if (CanEdit)
                {
                    <AppButton Variant="ButtonVariant.Secondary" Disabled="@ToolActionBusy" OnClick="OpenAddDialog" TestId="tools-add-open">
                        Add tool
                    </AppButton>
                    <AppButton Variant="ButtonVariant.Ghost" Disabled="@ToolActionBusy" OnClick="OpenImportDialog" TestId="tools-import-open">
                        Import
                    </AppButton>
                }
            </div>
        </div>;

    /// <summary>A kind tab's cards: one per catalog group, each with a fold.</summary>
    private RenderFragment KindBody(string tabId) =>
        @<section class="tools-kind" data-tool-kind="@KindTabOf(tabId)?.KindKey" data-testid="tools-kind" aria-label="@TabTitle(tabId)">
            @if (GroupsOn(tabId).Count == 0)
            {
                <EmptyState Title="@EmptyTabTitle(tabId)"
                            Description="@EmptyTabDescription(tabId)"
                            TestId="tools-kind-empty" />
            }
            @foreach (var group in GroupsOn(tabId))
            {
                <Card @key="group.Key" CssClass="tools-group" data-tool-group="@group.Key">
                    <div class="tools-group__fold-row">
                        <FoldControl CssClass="tools-group__fold"
                                     Expanded="@IsExpanded(group)"
                                     ExpandedChanged="expanded => SetExpanded(group, expanded)"
                                     Controls="@group.RegionId"
                                     HeadingLevel="3"
                                     Label="@GroupLabel(group)"
                                     TestId="tools-group-toggle" />
                        @if (group.Outstanding == 0)
                        {
                            <Badge Kind="tone" Slug="settled" CssClass="tools-group__status" TestId="tools-group-current">All up to date</Badge>
                        }
                    </div>
                    <DataTable TItem="DevToolInfo"
                               Columns="Columns"
                               Items="@group.Tools"
                               RowKey="ToolKey"
                               RowCssClass="RowClass"
                               RowAttributes="RowIdentity"
                               RowNoun="tool"
                               CssClass="tools-inventory"
                               AriaLabel="@group.AriaLabel(TabTitle(tabId))"
                               id="@group.RegionId"
                               hidden="@(!IsExpanded(group))">
                        <Row Context="tool">@ToolRow(tool)</Row>
                    </DataTable>
                </Card>
            }
        </section>;

    /// <summary>One row's cells. The cells are this pane's and the frame is the
    /// library's, which is the split DataTable is built around: the catalog key a
    /// driver scopes the shared per-row test ids by, and the quieting a switched-off
    /// row is drawn with, arrive through the component rather than through a tr
    /// this pane writes.</summary>
    private RenderFragment ToolRow(DevToolInfo tool) =>
        @<text>
            <td class="tools-inventory__tool">
                @* The name, and beside it one chip per host the row is for: which
                   hosts an entry is for decides what its Update actually does. An
                   application has no host at all — it is machine software, not a
                   registration with an AI tool — so it gets no chip rather than
                   one reading "No host". *@
                <span class="tools-inventory__name-line">
                    <strong>@tool.Name</strong>
                    @if (tool.Hosts is not DevToolHosts.None)
                    {
                        <span class="tools-inventory__host-chips" title="@HostTitle(tool)" data-testid="tools-row-hosts">
                            @foreach (var host in HostsOf(tool.Hosts))
                            {
                                <Badge BaseClass="tools-inventory__hosts" Kind="">@HostLabel(host)</Badge>
                            }
                        </span>
                    }
                </span>
                <span class="tools-inventory__meta">@StatusOf(tool)</span>
                @if (NoteOf(tool) is { Length: > 0 } note)
                {
                    @* The catalog's own warning, on a line of its own
                       rather than run together with the status. Several
                       of these are the only reason a row reads the way it
                       does — a version that legitimately reads backwards,
                       a probe that is a heuristic, a package that does not
                       exist — and appended to the status they were a
                       sentence nobody finished reading. *@
                    <span class="tools-inventory__note" data-testid="tools-row-note">@note</span>
                }
                @if (tool.CachedVersions.Count > 0)
                {
                    @* What Claude has kept of this plugin, under the row
                       rather than in a column: it is a list, and a fourth
                       column would be one more thing the narrow pane has
                       to stack. Each version says whether an install still
                       loads it, because that is the only fact that decides
                       whether it may go — and the one a person clearing
                       "old versions" by hand gets wrong: a project scope
                       registered months ago still points at the folder it
                       was made against, and deleting that one does not move
                       the project on, it breaks it. Those get no button. *@
                    <span class="tools-inventory__cache" data-testid="tools-row-cache">
                        <span class="tools-inventory__cache-label">Cached in Claude</span>
                        @foreach (var cached in tool.CachedVersions)
                        {
                            <span class="tools-inventory__cache-version @(cached.InUse ? "tools-inventory__cache-version--in-use" : "tools-inventory__cache-version--stale")"
                                  data-testid="tools-row-cache-version"
                                  data-version="@cached.Version"
                                  data-in-use="@(cached.InUse ? "true" : "false")"
                                  title="@cached.Path">
                                @cached.Version
                                <span class="tools-inventory__cache-use">@CacheUseLabel(cached)</span>
                                @if (!cached.InUse)
                                {
                                    <AppButton Variant="ButtonVariant.Ghost"
                                               Size="ButtonSize.Small"
                                               CssClass="tools-inventory__cache-remove"
                                               aria-label="@($"Remove cached {cached.Version} of {tool.Name}")"
                                               Disabled="@(_toolsLoading || ToolActionBusy)"
                                               TestId="tools-row-cache-remove"
                                               OnClick="() => AskToRemoveCachedVersion(tool, cached)">
                                        Remove
                                    </AppButton>
                                }
                            </span>
                        }
                    </span>
                }
            </td>
            @* Each version cell names itself. The row's four columns only
               fit while the pane is wide enough for them — it is a surface
               beside a workspace, so it often is not — and below that the
               row stacks and the heading row goes. Two bare version strings
               in a column say nothing about which is which, so the label is
               in the markup at every width and the stylesheet decides when
               it shows. *@
            <td class="tools-inventory__version" data-testid="tools-row-installed">
                <span class="tools-inventory__cell-label">Installed</span>@InstalledLabel(tool)
            </td>
            <td class="tools-inventory__version @(tool.CanUpdate ? "tools-inventory__version--new" : string.Empty)">
                <span class="tools-inventory__cell-label">Available</span>@tool.AvailableVersion
            </td>
            <td class="tools-inventory__status" data-testid="tools-row-status" data-state="@StateOf(tool)">
                <span class="tools-inventory__cell-label">Status</span><Badge Kind="tone" Slug="@StateTone(StateOf(tool))" CssClass="tools-inventory__status-chip">@StateLabel(StateOf(tool))</Badge>
            </td>
            <td class="tools-inventory__actions-cell">
                @* The grid the three slots sit in is an element inside
                   the cell rather than the cell itself: a `td` carrying
                   `display: grid` is no longer a table-cell box, so it
                   loses the row's vertical alignment and has the
                   library's bottom rule drawn around the grid instead of
                   at the row's edge. The same reasoning the library
                   records on `.data-table__group-heading`. *@
                <div class="tools-inventory__actions">
                    @* One slot per thing the row lets you do: the action
                       or the status, the toggle, and Remove. Each names
                       its own column in the stylesheet rather than taking
                       the one its order would give it, because a
                       marketplace has no toggle and a read-only host no
                       Remove — and under auto-placement the survivors
                       would slide into the empty track, misaligning
                       exactly the rows that differ from the ones around
                       them. Why the tracks are the widths they are is in
                       `.tools-inventory__actions`. *@
                    <span class="tools-inventory__action">
                        @if (tool.Kind is DevToolKind.Marketplace)
                        {
                            @* A marketplace has no version to be behind, so it
                               never earns an Update through CanUpdate the way a
                               tool does. What it has is two states — Claude
                               knows it or does not — and one verb for each. *@
                            <AppButton Variant="ButtonVariant.Secondary"
                                       Disabled="@(_toolsLoading || ToolActionBusy)"
                                       OnClick="() => ApplyToolActionAsync(tool, DevToolAction.Update)">
                                @(tool.Installed ? "Update" : "Add")
                            </AppButton>
                        }
                        else if (tool.ConfirmedByHand && tool.ConfiguredEnabled)
                        {
                            @* The one row whose control is not a verb. Nothing
                               can check it, so the only honest answer on it is
                               somebody's word — and a box, unlike a button, can
                               be taken back. It went through Update once, and
                               that was the defect: ticking it removed the only
                               control the row had, so a mis-click was permanent
                               short of hand-editing the per-PC file.

                               Only while the machine wants the row at all. A
                               switched-off row has nothing to confirm, and a
                               tick on one would be a statement about something
                               this machine has said it is not doing. *@
                            <Checkbox Checked="@tool.Acknowledged"
                                      CheckedChanged="acknowledged => AcknowledgeAsync(tool, acknowledged)"
                                      Disabled="@(_toolsLoading || ToolActionBusy)"
                                      Label="Done here"
                                      CssClass="tools-inventory__ack"
                                      TestId="tools-row-acknowledge" />
                        }
                        else if (tool.CanUpdate)
                        {
                            <AppButton Variant="ButtonVariant.Secondary"
                                       Disabled="@(_toolsLoading || ToolActionBusy)"
                                       OnClick="() => ApplyToolActionAsync(tool, DevToolAction.Update)">
                                Update
                            </AppButton>
                        }
                        else if (tool.CanInstall)
                        {
                            @* The same action as an update. The port has one
                               verb for "make this machine match the config",
                               and behind it the host already runs
                               "plugin install" or "tool install" for a tool
                               that is enabled and absent — what changes here
                               is only that the row now offers it.

                               CanInstall is false for a row with no mechanism
                               behind it, which is what keeps the checklist rows
                               and the install-by-hand packages from being given
                               a button with nothing to run. *@
                            <AppButton Variant="ButtonVariant.Secondary"
                                       Disabled="@(_toolsLoading || ToolActionBusy)"
                                       OnClick="() => ApplyToolActionAsync(tool, DevToolAction.Update)">
                                Install
                            </AppButton>
                        }
                        else if (tool.CanReRegister)
                        {
                            @* A registration that exists and points at the wrong
                               thing, which neither of the two branches above can
                               repair: both are gated on there being something to
                               install, and a server reached over HTTP or started
                               by a command has nothing.

                               The same verb, again. Update is the port's one word
                               for "make this machine match the config", and behind
                               it the host already removes and re-adds a
                               registration whose target differs — what changes
                               here is only that the row now offers it, and says
                               what pressing it does in terms of a registration
                               rather than of a version. *@
                            <AppButton Variant="ButtonVariant.Secondary"
                                       Disabled="@(_toolsLoading || ToolActionBusy)"
                                       OnClick="() => ApplyToolActionAsync(tool, DevToolAction.Update)"
                                       TestId="tools-row-reregister">
                                Re-register
                            </AppButton>
                        }
                        else
                        {
                            <span class="tools-inventory__action-note">@ActionNote(tool)</span>
                        }
                    </span>
                    @if (tool.Kind is not DevToolKind.Marketplace)
                    {
                        <span class="tools-inventory__toggle">
                            @* Not for a marketplace. It is not a tool this
                               machine opts into — it is where the Claude
                               plugins that do come from — so the only way to
                               stop wanting one is to remove it. *@
                            <AppButton Variant="ButtonVariant.Ghost"
                                       Disabled="@(_toolsLoading || ToolActionBusy)"
                                       OnClick="() => ApplyToolActionAsync(tool, tool.ConfiguredEnabled ? DevToolAction.Disable : DevToolAction.Enable)">
                                @(tool.ConfiguredEnabled ? "Disable" : "Enable")
                            </AppButton>
                        </span>
                    }
                    @if (CanEdit)
                    {
                        <span class="tools-inventory__remove">
                            @* The row's test id is shared by every row, so a driver
                               scopes it through the row's data-tool-key. One id per
                               row would be an id nobody could write a selector for
                               without knowing the catalog first. *@
                            <DeleteButton Label="Remove"
                                          AriaLabel="@($"Remove {tool.Name} from the catalog")"
                                          Size="ButtonSize.Small"
                                          ShowIcon="false"
                                          Disabled="@(_toolsLoading || ToolActionBusy)"
                                          TestId="tools-row-remove"
                                          OnClick="() => AskToRemove(tool)" />
                        </span>
                    }
                </div>
            </td>
        </text>;

